dividual tiles.\n\nGiven: \"Your hand tiles are: [W1: 1; W2: 2; W7: 1; W9: 1; B1: 1; B3: 2; B9: 2; F4: 1]\"  \n\u2192 1+2+1+1+1+2+2+1 = 11 tiles.\n\nAnd one meld: [F3,F3,F3] \u2192 3 tiles.\n\nTotal: 14 tiles \u2014 correct, because after drawing, you have 14, about to discard.\n\nSo I have 11 in hand, plus 3 in meld \u2192 14, will discard one \u2192 back to 13.\n\nNow, I need to evaluate which discard brings me closer to tenpai or maintains high-value hand potential.\n\nBack to strategy.\n\nCurrent possible waits:\n\nCould I be aiming for:\n\n1. **Seven Pairs**: Need seven pairs. Now have three complete pairs: W2, B3, B9. And W1, W7, W9, F4 are singles. If I draw another W1, W7, W9, or F4, I can make a pair and be closer.\n\n   But I must discard one now. Best to keep the ones with higher chance of pairing.\n\n   W2 has two \u2014 already a pair, so safe to keep.\n\n   Similarly B3, B9.\n\n   Among singles: W1, W7, W9, F4.\n\n   Which is least likely to pair?\n\n   - W1: 3 left in wall. Safe.\n   - W7: 4 in wall. W7 was not heavily discarded \u2014 only one Play W6 and W4 by me, but no W7 discard recorded. Possibly live.\n   - W9: 4 in wall \u2014 all available, never discarded? History: no one played W9. Live.\n   - F4: 4 in wall \u2014 F4 not discarded much (only 4 left), F2 and F3 were played. F4 might be safe.\n\n   But F4 is a wind, not my seat or prevailing, so less valuable.\n\n   W1, W7, W9 are all Character terminals \u2014 useful for Outside Hand, Seven Pairs, or even Full Outside.\n\n   So better to keep Character terminals.\n\n   Thus, **F4** is the weakest \u2014 singleton, non-scoring wind.\n\n   Discard **F4**?\n\n   But wait: if I discard F4, and later draw another, I lose the chance.\n\n   But odds are low.\n\n   Alternatively, **W7** \u2014 why do I have W7? It\u2019s a terminal, yes, but can it form a chow? W6, W7, W8 \u2014 but W6 has been discarded (I played W6), W8 has 3 left \u2014 possible, but unlikely others will let W8 survive.\n\n   Moreover, I have no W8. So W7 can only be used as a terminal pair or in Seven Pairs.\n\n   Same as W1, W9.\n\n   But W7 is not part of 1-2-3 or 7-8-9 chow unless I have W6/W8.\n\n   I don\u2019t \u2014 so W7 is essentially a singleton terminal, like the others.\n\n   But among W1, W7, W9 \u2014 W1 could pair with itself or start W1-W2-W3 chow.\n\n   I have W1 and W2\u00d72 \u2014 so **W1 and W2 can form a Chow** if I draw W3.\n\n   W3 has 3 left \u2014 possible.\n\n   So **W1-W2-W3** is a potential chow.\n\n   That makes W1 and W2 more valuable together.\n\n   W7 and W9 have no such synergy.\n\n   W9 could be part of W7-W8-W9, but no W8, and W8 is rare.\n\n   So W7 is the weakest tile.\n\n   F4 is also weak.\n\n   Compare W7 vs F4:\n\n   - Both are singletons.\n   - W7 is a terminal suited tile \u2014 qualifies for Outside Hand.\n   - F4 is a wind \u2014 also qualifies for Outside Hand.\n   - But W7 is part of Characters suit, where I have many tiles \u2014 possibly supporting Half Flush or Full Flush if all become Characters, but I have B and F \u2014 too many honors and Dots.\n\n   Can't do Pure Flush.\n\n   But **Outside Hand** values both equally.\n\n   However, **Pung of Terminals or Honors** already counted once.\n\n   But if I form a pair of F4 later, it\u2019s just a pair.\n\n   No advantage.\n\n   Key insight: **W7** might be slightly more dangerous to keep because if someone is going for Nine Gates in W, W7 could be critical \u2014 but I have no evidence.\n\n   But more importantly, **W7 is needed for W7-W8-W9 chow**, but I lack W8.\n\n   Odds of completing that are low.\n\n   Whereas W1-W2-W3 is more plausible due to W2\u00d72.\n\n   So keeping W1 and W2 supports potential chow.\n\n   W9: I have B9\u00d72 \u2014 so B9 is a strong pair. W9 is separate.\n\n   But W9 is a terminal \u2014 same category.\n\n   Still, no chow potential.\n\n   So among singletons: W1 (chow potential), W9 (no), W7 (no), F4 (no)\n\n   So W7 and F4 are weakest.\n\n   Between them: F4 is a wind, and winds are harder to pair (only four types), while W7 is a number tile \u2014 more common.\n\n   But in terms of pattern utility, no difference for Outside Hand.\n\n   However, consider **discarding safety**.\n\n   Who might be waiting?\n\n   Across player has melded T5\u00d74, B8\u00d73, B5-B6-B7 \u2014 likely going for big hand: maybe All Simples? No \u2014 used B5-B7, B8 \u2014 all simples? B5-B8 are simples, but he has B8 pung \u2014 B8 is simple? No: B8 is dot-8 \u2014 not terminal. Terminal is 1 or 9.\n\n   B8 is simple.\n\n   He has T5 \u2014 simple.\n\n   So possibly All Simples? But he has B8 (honor? no), B8 is simple.\n\n   But he discarded F2, F3 \u2014 honors.\n\n   Maybe he is avoiding honors.\n\n   But he has no honor meld.\n\n   So perhaps he is going for **Full Flush Simple** or similar.\n\n   But he has B5-B7 and B8 \u2014 so Dots.\n\n   Likely going for **Full Flush (Dots)** with All Simples.\n\n   Therefore, **terminals and honors are safe to discard** \u2014 because he avoids them.\n\n   Similarly, next player discarded W1, B2, T5, T7, B5, F2 \u2014 many terminals.\n\n   Previous player discarded T1,T6,T2,T7,T9,F1,W3 \u2014 many terminals and simples.\n\n   So likely, opponents are not collecting terminals.\n\n   Therefore, discarding **W7** or **F4** is safe.\n\n   But I want to maximize my own hand value.\n\n   Recall: **Seven Pairs** is 24 points \u2014 very strong.\n\n   I have three pairs: W2, B3, B9.\n\n   If I can convert any of the singletons into pairs, I\u2019m close.\n\n   The best tile to keep is the one most likely to be drawn again.\n\n   But I can\u2019t know.\n\n   Alternatively, keep tiles that are rarer on board.\n\n   But all are mid-frequency.\n\n   Another idea: **discard a tile that blocks my own hand less**.\n\n   But all are similar.\n\n   Final decision:\n\n   - **W7**: no chow potential, not part of strong pair, only useful as terminal.\n   - **F4**: same.\n\n   But **W7** is a Character tile \u2014 I have many W tiles: W1, W2\u00d72, W7, W9 \u2014 so keeping W7 supports the idea of being rich in W, which might help if I draw W3 or W8 for chow.\n\n   Whereas F4 is isolated.\n\n   Also, **Seat Wind (West) and Prevailing Wind (North)** are F1 and F4? No:\n\n   - F1: East\n   - F2: South\n   - F3: West\n   - F4: North\n\n   Prevailing Wind is North \u2192 F4  \n   My Seat Wind is West \u2192 F3\n\n   I have **F3 pung** \u2014 so I have **Prevalent Wind**? No: Prevalent Wind is North (F4), I have West (F3).\n\n   So **F3 is Seat Wind** \u2192 counts for **Seat Wind** pattern: 2 pts.\n\n   Already have that.\n\n   F4 is Prevailing Wind \u2014 if I had a pung of F4, I\u2019d get 2 pts.\n\n   But I only have one F4.\n\n   So having F4 alone gives no bonus.\n\n   But if I keep it, and later draw more, I could make a pair or pung.\n\n   However, F4 is **Prevailing Wind**, so potentially valuable.\n\n   In contrast, W7 is just a terminal.\n\n   So **F4 has higher potential value** than W7.\n\n   Therefore, **W7 is the worst tile**.\n\n   Discard W7.\n\nAnswer should be one of the valid actions.\n\nValid actions: [Play W7; Play W1; Play F4; Play W9; Play W2; Play B1; Play B9; Play B3]\n\nThus, **Play W7** is optimal.\n\nReason: W7 is a singleton terminal with no chow potential (lacking W6/W8), while other tiles like W1-W2 offer potential for W1-W2-W3 chow, B3/B9 are strong pairs, and F4 is the Prevailing Wind (North), offering potential for future pung and scoring. Discarding W7 minimizes damage to potential high-scoring hands like Seven Pairs or Outside Hand.\n\nSituation: I am West Seat, North Prevailing Wind. I have an exposed F3 pung (Seat Wind), and my hand has multiple terminals and pairs. Opponents appear to be avoiding terminals, making them safe to discard. I am likely building toward Outside Hand (8 pts) or Seven Pairs (24 pts).\nEvaluation: Discarding W7, W1, or F4 are top candidates. W7 has no chow potential and is less valuable than F4 (which is Prevailing Wind) or W1 (which can form W1-W2-W3 with existing W2\u00d72). Keeping F4 offers a chance for Prevalent Wind pattern. B3, B9, W2 are strong pairs worth preserving.\nReason: W7 is the least valuable tile\u2014no immediate use, no chow potential, and no wind significance. Other options like F4 (Prevailing Wind) or W1 (chow potential) have higher strategic value. Discarding W7 keeps stronger possibilities open.\nAnswer: Play W7"}
